            example: `
    <h3>Practical Examples: Characteristics of a Computer</h3>

    <p>Imagine that you are preparing a school project on your computer. Let us see how its different characteristics help you complete the project.</p>

    <h4>1. Speed</h4>
    <p>You need to type five pages for your project. A computer allows you to type, edit, and arrange the pages quickly. You can also copy and paste information when appropriate.</p>
    <p><strong>How it helps:</strong> You can complete your work in less time.</p>

    <h4>2. Accuracy</h4>
    <p>Your project includes a mathematics question: 25 + 15. When you enter the calculation correctly into a calculator program, it displays 40.</p>
    <p><strong>How it helps:</strong> The computer can calculate accurately when the input and instructions are correct.</p>

    <h4>3. Diligence</h4>
    <p>You need to repeat the same calculation for 100 questions. The computer can perform the calculation repeatedly without getting tired or bored.</p>
    <p><strong>How it helps:</strong> Repetitive tasks can be completed without human fatigue affecting the process.</p>

    <h4>4. Storage</h4>
    <p>You save your project in a folder named "Science Project." The next day, you open the saved file and continue working from where you stopped.</p>
    <p><strong>How it helps:</strong> Your work remains available for future use, provided the file has been saved and the storage device is accessible.</p>

    <h4>5. Automation</h4>
    <p>You create a presentation about planets and set each slide to change automatically after five seconds.</p>
    <p><strong>How it helps:</strong> The presentation follows your settings without requiring you to change every slide manually.</p>

    <h4>6. Versatility</h4>
    <p>You use the same computer to write your project, draw a diagram of the solar system, watch a science video, and practise mathematics.</p>
    <p><strong>How it helps:</strong> One computer can support many different activities.</p>

    <h4>7. Reliability</h4>
    <p>You use a properly working computer to save your project and calculate the marks for a classroom activity. You can use it again to perform the same tasks when needed.</p>
    <p><strong>How it helps:</strong> A properly maintained computer can perform tasks consistently. Saving backup copies helps protect important work.</p>

    <h4>8. Multitasking</h4>
    <p>You listen to an educational audio lesson while typing notes and downloading a worksheet.</p>
    <p><strong>How it helps:</strong> The computer can handle several activities during the same period.</p>

    <h4>9. Internet Connectivity</h4>
    <p>You connect your computer to the internet and search for trustworthy information about the planets. You can also attend an online science class.</p>
    <p><strong>How it helps:</strong> You can access online learning resources and communicate with your teacher.</p>

    <h4>10. Consistency</h4>
    <p>You calculate 100 + 200 using the same calculator program several times. Under the same conditions, it gives the result 300 each time.</p>
    <p><strong>How it helps:</strong> A computer can repeat the same operation and produce consistent results.</p>

    <h4>11. Programmability</h4>
    <p>You use a beginner-friendly programming tool to create a small game. You write instructions that tell a character to move when you press an arrow key.</p>
    <p><strong>How it helps:</strong> Programs allow a computer to perform tasks according to instructions written for a particular purpose.</p>

    <h4>12. Limits of Computer Intelligence</h4>
    <p>Your computer can calculate your marks, but your teacher can better understand why you found a question difficult and suggest how you can improve.</p>
    <p><strong>How it helps:</strong> Computers support our work, while human understanding and judgment remain important.</p>

    <h4>Classroom Activity: Match the Characteristics</h4>
    <p>Match each situation with the correct characteristic.</p>
    <ol>
      <li>A computer calculates 500 marksheets quickly.</li>
      <li>You save your homework and open it tomorrow.</li>
      <li>A computer repeats a calculation without getting tired.</li>
      <li>You listen to music while typing a document.</li>
      <li>A slideshow changes pictures automatically.</li>
      <li>You use one computer for drawing, studying, and writing.</li>
    </ol>
    <p><strong>Answers:</strong> 1. Speed, 2. Storage, 3. Diligence, 4. Multitasking, 5. Automation, 6. Versatility.</p>

    <h4>Think and Answer</h4>
    <ol>
      <li>What is the difference between speed and accuracy?</li>
      <li>Why does a computer sometimes produce incorrect results?</li>
      <li>Which characteristic helps us save a school project?</li>
      <li>What is meant by multitasking?</li>
      <li>Does every computer activity require an internet connection?</li>
      <li>Why should we keep backup copies of important files?</li>
    </ol>

    <h4>Remember</h4>
    <p>The characteristics of a computer help us perform tasks quickly, accurately, and consistently. We should provide correct instructions, save our work, protect our information, and use computers responsibly.</p>
  `,

