            question: "Types of Keys",
            answer: `
    <h3>What Are the Types of Keys on a Keyboard?</h3>
    <p>A computer keyboard has many keys, and each key performs a particular function. These keys help us type letters, numbers, symbols, and instructions into a computer.</p>

    <p>Keyboard keys are divided into different types according to their functions. Learning about these keys helps us use a computer easily and quickly.</p>

    <h3>1. Alphabet Keys</h3>
    <p><strong>Alphabet keys</strong> are used to type letters from A to Z. A standard keyboard has 26 alphabet keys.</p>
    <ul>
      <li>They help us type words, sentences, stories, and paragraphs.</li>
      <li>We can type uppercase letters and lowercase letters.</li>
      <li>Example: Computer, School, Book, and Teacher.</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>2. Number Keys</h3>
    <p><strong>Number keys</strong> are used to type numbers from 0 to 9. They are usually found in the top row of the keyboard. Many full-size keyboards also have a separate numeric keypad on the right side.</p>
    <ul>
      <li>They help us enter numbers and perform calculations.</li>
      <li>They can be used to type dates, ages, marks, and prices.</li>
      <li>Example: 12345, 2026, and 500.</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>3. Function Keys</h3>
    <p><strong>Function keys</strong> are labeled F1 to F12 and are usually found at the top of the keyboard. Their functions depend on the operating system and the program being used.</p>
    <table>
      <tr>
        <td><strong>Key</strong></td>
        <td><strong>Common Use</strong></td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>F1</td>
        <td>Often opens Help.</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>F2</td>
        <td>Can rename a selected file in Windows File Explorer.</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>F5</td>
        <td>Often refreshes a webpage or window.</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>F11</td>
        <td>Often turns full-screen mode on or off in a web browser.</td>
      </tr>
    </table>

    <h3>4. Control Keys</h3>
    <p><strong>Control keys</strong> help us control the computer or perform special actions. Some common control keys are Ctrl, Alt, Shift, Esc, and the Windows key.</p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Ctrl:</strong> Works with other keys to perform shortcuts, such as Ctrl + C to copy.</li>
      <li><strong>Alt:</strong> Works with other keys to perform special actions.</li>
      <li><strong>Shift:</strong> Helps type capital letters and the upper symbols printed on keys.</li>
      <li><strong>Esc:</strong> Often cancels an operation or closes a menu.</li>
      <li><strong>Windows Key:</strong> Usually opens the Start menu on a Windows computer.</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>5. Navigation Keys</h3>
    <p><strong>Navigation keys</strong> help us move through text, documents, and webpages.</p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Arrow Keys:</strong> Move the cursor up, down, left, or right.</li>
      <li><strong>Home:</strong> Often moves the cursor to the beginning of a line.</li>
      <li><strong>End:</strong> Often moves the cursor to the end of a line.</li>
      <li><strong>Page Up:</strong> Moves up by approximately one screen in many programs.</li>
      <li><strong>Page Down:</strong> Moves down by approximately one screen in many programs.</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>6. Special-Purpose Keys</h3>
    <p>These keys perform specific tasks while typing or using a computer.</p>
    <table>
      <tr>
        <td><strong>Key</strong></td>
        <td><strong>Use</strong></td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>Spacebar</td>
        <td>Creates a space between words.</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>Enter</td>
        <td>Starts a new line or confirms an action.</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>Backspace</td>
        <td>Deletes the character to the left of the cursor.</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>Delete</td>
        <td>Deletes the character to the right of the cursor or a selected item.</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>Tab</td>
        <td>Moves to the next field or adds an indent in some programs.</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>Caps Lock</td>
        <td>Turns uppercase typing on or off.</td>
      </tr>
    </table>

    <h3>7. Numeric Keypad Keys</h3>
    <p>A <strong>numeric keypad</strong> is a group of number and mathematical-operation keys found on many full-size keyboards. It makes entering numbers faster.</p>
    <ul>
      <li>Number keys enter digits.</li>
      <li>The plus (+) key is used for addition.</li>
      <li>The minus (-) key is used for subtraction.</li>
      <li>The multiplication (*) and division (/) keys are used for calculations.</li>
      <li>The Num Lock key controls number-entry mode on many numeric keypads.</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>8. Toggle Keys</h3>
    <p><strong>Toggle keys</strong> switch a setting on or off when pressed. Common examples include Caps Lock, Num Lock, and Scroll Lock.</p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Caps Lock:</strong> Turns uppercase typing on or off.</li>
      <li><strong>Num Lock:</strong> Changes the function of keys on many numeric keypads.</li>
      <li><strong>Scroll Lock:</strong> Changes scrolling behavior in certain programs, although it is rarely used today.</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>9. Shortcut Keys</h3>
    <p><strong>Shortcut keys</strong> are individual keys or combinations of keys that help us perform tasks quickly.</p>
    <table>
      <tr>
        <td><strong>Shortcut</strong></td>
        <td><strong>Function</strong></td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>Ctrl + C</td>
        <td>Copies selected content.</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>Ctrl + V</td>
        <td>Pastes copied or cut content.</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>Ctrl + X</td>
        <td>Cuts selected content.</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>Ctrl + Z</td>
        <td>Undoes the last action in many programs.</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>Ctrl + S</td>
        <td>Saves your work in many programs.</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>Ctrl + A</td>
        <td>Selects all content in many programs.</td>
      </tr>
    </table>

    <h3>10. Difference Between the Main Types of Keys</h3>
    <table>
      <tr>
        <td><strong>Type of Key</strong></td>
        <td><strong>Main Purpose</strong></td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>Alphabet Keys</td>
        <td>Type letters and words.</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>Number Keys</td>
        <td>Type numbers.</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>Function Keys</td>
        <td>Perform program-specific actions.</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>Control Keys</td>
        <td>Control actions and work with other keys.</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>Navigation Keys</td>
        <td>Move through text and documents.</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>Special-Purpose Keys</td>
        <td>Perform tasks such as spacing, deleting, and starting a new line.</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>Numeric Keypad Keys</td>
        <td>Enter numbers and mathematical operations quickly.</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>Toggle Keys</td>
        <td>Switch certain keyboard settings on or off.</td>
      </tr>
    </table>

    <h3>Summary</h3>
    <p>A keyboard contains different types of keys, such as alphabet keys, number keys, function keys, control keys, navigation keys, and special-purpose keys. Each type helps us perform different tasks. Understanding these keys makes computer use easier, faster, and more accurate.</p>
  `,
