            question: "Error Handling with Try/Catch",
            answer: "try/catch handles errors that are thrown while executing code. The try block contains code that may fail, and catch receives the thrown error. finally can run cleanup code regardless of whether an error occurred. To handle a rejected promise, use await inside try/catch or attach catch() to the promise.",
            example: "try {\n  const data = JSON.parse(\"{invalid}\");\n  console.log(data);\n} catch (error) {\n  console.error(\"Invalid JSON:\", error.message);\n} finally {\n  console.log(\"Parsing attempt finished.\");\n}",
