            id: 8,
            question: "Pointers and Interfaces",
            answer: "A pointer stores the memory address of a value. The & operator obtains an address, and the * operator accesses the value through a pointer. An interface defines behavior through a set of methods; a type implements an interface implicitly by providing those methods.",
            example: "package main\n\nimport \"fmt\"\n\ntype Speaker interface {\n    Speak() string\n}\n\ntype Person struct {\n    Name string\n}\n\nfunc (p Person) Speak() string {\n    return \"Hello, I am \" + p.Name\n}\n\nfunc updateAge(age *int) {\n    *age = 26\n}\n\nfunc main() {\n    age := 25\n    updateAge(&age)\n\n    var speaker Speaker = Person{Name: \"Ali\"}\n    fmt.Println(age)\n    fmt.Println(speaker.Speak())\n}",
