            id: 5,
            question: "Ownership",
            answer: "Ownership is Rust's system for managing memory safely. Each value has one owner, and when the owner goes out of scope, the value is normally dropped. Assigning a value can move ownership to another variable. Types such as integers that implement Copy are copied instead of moved.",
            example: "fn main() {\n    let first = String::from(\"Rust\");\n    let second = first; // Ownership moves to second.\n\n    // println!(\"{first}\"); // Error: first no longer owns the String.\n    println!(\"{second}\");\n\n    let x = 10;\n    let y = x; // Integers implement Copy.\n    println!(\"x = {x}, y = {y}\");\n}",
            image: "",
