            question: "Borrowing and References",
            answer: "Borrowing lets code use a value without taking ownership of it. An immutable reference, written as &, allows reading a value. A mutable reference, written as &mut, allows modification when Rust's borrowing rules are satisfied. Rust prevents conflicting mutable and immutable references to protect memory safety.",
            example: "fn print_name(name: &String) {\n    println!(\"Name: {name}\");\n}\n\nfn add_suffix(name: &mut String) {\n    name.push_str(\" Developer\");\n}\n\nfn main() {\n    let mut name = String::from(\"Abubakar\");\n\n    print_name(&name);\n    add_suffix(&mut name);\n\n    println!(\"Updated: {name}\");\n}",
