            id: 8,
            question: "Protocols and Extensions",
            answer: "A protocol defines properties and methods that a type must provide. Structures, classes, and enums can conform to protocols. Extensions add new functionality to existing types without changing their original definition, including adding computed properties and methods.",
            example: "protocol Greetable {\n    var name: String { get }\n    func greet() -> String\n}\n\nstruct Person: Greetable {\n    let name: String\n\n    func greet() -> String {\n        return \"Hello, \\(name)!\"\n    }\n}\n\nextension String {\n    var isNotEmpty: Bool {\n        return !isEmpty\n    }\n}\n\nlet person = Person(name: \"Ali\")\nprint(person.greet())\nprint(\"Swift\".isNotEmpty)",
            image: "",
