            id: 10,
            question: "SDLC Phases with a Real Example",
            answer: "The SDLC phases work together to take a software idea from planning to long-term support. A typical sequence is Planning → Requirement Analysis → Software Design → Coding and Implementation → Testing → Deployment → Maintenance. In Agile and other iterative approaches, teams may repeat several phases in short cycles rather than completing each phase only once.",
            example: "Project: Online School Management System\n\n1. Planning: Decide the project scope, budget, and timeline.\n2. Requirement Analysis: Gather requirements from teachers, students, and administrators.\n3. Software Design: Design the user interface, database, and system architecture.\n4. Coding and Implementation: Build login, student records, attendance, and reports.\n5. Testing: Verify features, security, and performance.\n6. Deployment: Launch the system for the school.\n7. Maintenance: Fix bugs, improve performance, and add requested features.\n\nResult: The school gets a working application that can be improved over time.",
