        heading: "Object-Oriented Design Principles",
        slug: "",
        qa: [
          {
            id: 1,
            question: "Classes and Objects",
            answer: "A class is a blueprint that defines the properties and methods of a type of object. An object is an instance of a class that contains data and can perform actions defined by that class. Classes and objects are fundamental concepts in object-oriented programming (OOP).",
            example: "In JavaScript:\nclass Student {\n  constructor(name, age) {\n    this.name = name;\n    this.age = age;\n  }\n\n  introduce() {\n    return `My name is ${this.name}`;\n  }\n}\n\nconst student1 = new Student(\"Ali\", 15);\nconsole.log(student1.introduce());",
            image: "",
            video: "",
            example_image: ""
          },
          {
            id: 2,
            question: "Encapsulation",
            answer: "Encapsulation is the OOP principle of keeping data and the methods that operate on it together while controlling access to internal details. It helps protect an object's state and prevents invalid changes. In JavaScript, private class fields can be declared using the # symbol.",
            example: "class BankAccount {\n  #balance = 0;\n\n  deposit(amount) {\n    if (amount <= 0) {\n      throw new Error(\"Amount must be positive\");\n    }\n    this.#balance += amount;\n  }\n\n  getBalance() {\n    return this.#balance;\n  }\n}\n\nconst account = new BankAccount();\naccount.deposit(500);\nconsole.log(account.getBalance()); // 500",
            image: "",
            video: "",
            example_image: ""
          },
          {
            id: 3,
            question: "Abstraction",
            answer: "Abstraction means exposing essential functionality while hiding unnecessary implementation details. It allows developers to use a feature without needing to understand every internal step. Abstraction reduces complexity and makes software easier to use and maintain.",
            example: "When using a car, a driver presses the accelerator to increase speed without needing to understand every detail of the engine. In software, a `sendEmail()` function can hide the internal steps of connecting to a server and sending a message.",
            image: "",
            video: "",
            example_image: ""
          },
          {
            id: 4,
            question: "Inheritance",
            answer: "Inheritance allows one class to extend another class and reuse or specialize its properties and methods. The original class is called the parent or base class, while the extending class is called the child or derived class. Inheritance can reduce duplication when a genuine 'is-a' relationship exists.",
            example: "class Animal {\n  eat() {\n    return \"Eating food\";\n  }\n}\n\nclass Dog extends Animal {\n  bark() {\n    return \"Woof!\";\n  }\n}\n\nconst dog = new Dog();\nconsole.log(dog.eat());  // Eating food\nconsole.log(dog.bark()); // Woof!",
            image: "",
            video: "",
            example_image: ""
          },
          {
            id: 5,
            question: "Polymorphism",
            answer: "Polymorphism means that different objects can provide different implementations of the same operation or interface. It allows developers to write flexible code that works with multiple object types without needing to know each object's exact implementation.",
            example: "class Shape {\n  area() {\n    throw new Error(\"Implement area()\");\n  }\n}\n\nclass Circle extends Shape {\n  constructor(radius) {\n    super();\n    this.radius = radius;\n  }\n\n  area() {\n    return Math.PI * this.radius ** 2;\n  }\n}\n\nclass Square extends Shape {\n  constructor(side) {\n    super();\n    this.side = side;\n  }\n\n  area() {\n    return this.side ** 2;\n  }\n}\n\nconst shapes = [new Circle(3), new Square(4)];\nshapes.forEach(shape => console.log(shape.area()));",
            image: "",
            video: "",
            example_image: ""
          },
          {
            id: 6,
            question: "Composition vs Inheritance",
            answer: "Inheritance models an 'is-a' relationship, where a class extends another class. Composition models a 'has-a' or 'uses-a' relationship by combining objects with specific responsibilities. Composition often makes systems more flexible because behavior can be changed by replacing or combining components rather than creating deep class hierarchies.",
            example: "Inheritance example: `class Dog extends Animal {}` because a dog is an animal. Composition example: a `Car` has an `Engine` object and uses its `start()` method. If different cars need different engines, composition can make those choices easier to change.",
            image: "",
            video: "",
            example_image: ""
          },
          {
            id: 7,
            question: "SOLID Principles",
            answer: "SOLID is a set of five object-oriented design principles that help create maintainable and extensible software. S means Single Responsibility Principle, O means Open/Closed Principle, L means Liskov Substitution Principle, I means Interface Segregation Principle, and D means Dependency Inversion Principle.",
            example: "Single Responsibility: keep invoice calculation separate from email delivery. Open/Closed: add a new payment method through an extension point rather than repeatedly changing stable code. Liskov Substitution: a subtype should behave as its base type promises. Interface Segregation: prefer focused interfaces over one large interface. Dependency Inversion: high-level business logic should depend on abstractions instead of specific low-level implementations.",
            image: "",
            video: "",
            example_image: ""
          },
          {
            id: 8,
            question: "Dependency Injection",
            answer: "Dependency Injection (DI) is a technique in which an object receives the services or dependencies it needs from outside instead of creating them internally. DI reduces tight coupling and makes software easier to test because dependencies can be replaced with alternative implementations or test doubles.",
            example: "class UserService {\n  constructor(userRepository) {\n    this.userRepository = userRepository;\n  }\n\n  getUser(id) {\n    return this.userRepository.findById(id);\n  }\n}\n\nconst mockRepository = {\n  findById: id => ({ id, name: \"Ali\" })\n};\n\nconst service = new UserService(mockRepository);\nconsole.log(service.getUser(1));",
            image: "",
            video: "",
            example_image: ""
          },
          {
            id: 9,
            question: "Interfaces and Abstraction",
            answer: "An interface defines a contract describing the operations a component must provide without requiring callers to know its implementation. Abstraction is the broader idea of exposing essential behavior and hiding implementation details. Languages such as TypeScript and Java support explicit interfaces, while JavaScript commonly uses documented contracts and compatible object shapes.",
            example: "In TypeScript:\ninterface NotificationService {\n  send(message: string): void;\n}\n\nclass EmailNotification implements NotificationService {\n  send(message: string): void {\n    console.log(`Email sent: ${message}`);\n  }\n}\n\nconst notifier: NotificationService = new EmailNotification();\nnotifier.send(\"Welcome!\");",
            image: "",
            video: "",
            example_image: ""
          },
          {
            id: 10,
            question: "Designing Reusable Components",
            answer: "Designing reusable components means creating independent, focused parts of a system that can be used in multiple places. Good reusable components have clear responsibilities, well-defined inputs and outputs, minimal unnecessary dependencies, and consistent behavior. They should be reusable without becoming overly complex or full of unrelated options.",
            example: "A reusable React Button component:\nfunction Button({ label, onClick, disabled = false }) {\n  return (\n    <button onClick={onClick} disabled={disabled}>\n      {label}\n    </button>\n  );\n}\n\n// Usage:\n<Button label=\"Save\" onClick={handleSave} />\n<Button label=\"Cancel\" onClick={handleCancel} />",
            image: "",
            video: "",
            example_image: ""
          }
        ]
