          { id: 8, question: "What Are Normalization and Data Redundancy?", answer: "Normalization organizes relational tables to reduce unnecessary duplication and update problems. Data redundancy means storing repeated copies of the same information.", example: "Store teacher details once and refer to the teacher by ID rather than repeating all details in every class record.", image: "", video: "", example_image: "" },
          { id: 9, question: "What Are Transactions and ACID Properties?", answer: "A transaction groups database operations. ACID stands for Atomicity, Consistency, Isolation, and Durability, properties that help ensure reliable transaction processing.", example: "A bank transfer should not subtract money from one account without correctly applying the corresponding deposit.", image: "", video: "", example_image: "" },
          { id: 10, question: "What Are NoSQL Databases?", answer: "NoSQL databases use data models such as documents, key-value pairs, wide-column stores, or graphs rather than relying only on relational tables.", example: "A document database may store a user profile as a JSON-like document.", image: "", video: "", example_image: "" }
